{
  "children": [
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Proposal: Quantum error correction in superconducting circuits",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "type": "Title"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Dr A. Researcher — Department of Quantum Engineering",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "type": "Author"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Project summary",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "This proposal requests funding for a three-year programme to develop and experimentally validate a new family of quantum error-correcting codes tailored to the noise characteristics of transmon-based superconducting circuits. The expected outcomes are (i) a code family with provably lower overhead than the surface code for biased noise, (ii) a prototype implementation on a 20-qubit device, and (iii) open-source software for simulating and benchmarking the codes.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Background and motivation",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Quantum error correction (QEC) is widely regarded as essential for practical quantum computing ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "keys": [
            "terhal2015",
            "campbell2017"
          ],
          "style": "citep",
          "type": "Citation"
        },
        {
          "text": ". The surface code ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "keys": [
            "fowler2012"
          ],
          "style": "citep",
          "type": "Citation"
        },
        {
          "text": " is the current front-runner because of its high threshold and local stabiliser checks. However, its overhead — roughly ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "O(d^2)",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " physical qubits per logical qubit for code distance ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "d",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " — remains prohibitive for near-term hardware.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Recent theoretical work has shown that when the physical noise is biased (i.e. phase-flip errors dominate bit-flip errors, as is typical for transmons at long coherence times), tailored codes can achieve the same logical error rate with significantly fewer qubits ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "keys": [
            "tuckett2020",
            "dua2024"
          ],
          "style": "citep",
          "type": "Citation"
        },
        {
          "text": ". This project aims to move those theoretical gains into the laboratory.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Objectives",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "ordered": true,
      "items": [
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Design a family of bias-tailored stabiliser codes optimised for the noise profile of state-of-the-art transmon qubits.",
              "marks": [],
              "type": "Text"
            }
          ],
          "type": "ListItem"
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Develop an open-source decoder that runs within the real-time feedback latency budget of current cryogenic electronics (< 1 μs per syndrome round).",
              "marks": [],
              "type": "Text"
            }
          ],
          "type": "ListItem"
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Implement the best candidate code on a 20-qubit superconducting device and measure the logical error rate at distances 3, 5 and 7.",
              "marks": [],
              "type": "Text"
            }
          ],
          "type": "ListItem"
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Benchmark the code against the surface code under identical hardware conditions.",
              "marks": [],
              "type": "Text"
            }
          ],
          "type": "ListItem"
        }
      ],
      "type": "List"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Methodology",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "level": 2,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Code construction",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "We will use a computer search over the space of CSS codes with biased distance, parameterised by the physical noise bias ratio ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "\\eta = p_Z / p_X",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": ". The search objective is to minimise the number of physical qubits ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "n",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " subject to a target logical error rate:",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "latex": "p_L \\leq p_{\\text{phys}}^{\\lfloor d/2 \\rfloor}\\binom{d}{\\lfloor d/2 \\rfloor}",
      "numbered": true,
      "label": "eq:threshold",
      "type": "MathBlock"
    },
    {
      "level": 2,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Decoder design",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "We will adapt the Union-Find decoder ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "keys": [
            "delfosse2021"
          ],
          "style": "citep",
          "type": "Citation"
        },
        {
          "text": " to exploit the noise bias. The key modification is a weighted growth rule that expands clusters anisotropically in the Tanner graph, reducing the average number of growth steps and thus the wall-clock decoding time.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 2,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Experimental validation",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "The experimental work will be carried out on the 20-qubit device in our laboratory. Each code instance will be run for ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "10^6",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " syndrome rounds, and the logical error rate will be extracted by majority-vote decoding of repeated stabiliser measurements.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
